  "response": "Separating an introduced soil-borne pathogen from prolonged water deficit as the cause of a stand-level crown dieback requires evidence that only one of the two explanations can generate. The investigation therefore begins with an attempt to recover and name a causal organism rather than with symptom description alone. Soil and rhizosphere samples are baited with young rhododendron or pear leaves to recover Phytophthora from the stand, and this recovery in turn leads to sequencing of the internal transcribed spacer region of ribosomal DNA, which identifies the isolate to species level. Subsequently, a pathogenicity trial is mounted: inoculation of healthy oaks with the isolate reproduced the dieback symptoms, and the same organism was recovered from those inoculated trees. In other words, Koch's postulates were fulfilled for the oak isolate.\n\nThe second line of evidence is the match between the biology of the organism and the lesions found on the trees themselves. Zoospore production and root infection by Phytophthora require water-saturated soil conditions. Root infection by the organism produces necrotic lesions on fine roots and necrosis of the inner bark at the root collar. Tarry or rust-coloured bleeding cankers at the stem base are a diagnostic sign of Phytophthora collar rot, and surveyors record their height and circumferential extent on every affected stem. It is also held that Phytophthora zoospores are dispersed primarily by wind currents over hundreds of kilometres, and this account of long-distance aerial spread cannot be squared with the strict dependence of zoospore production and root infection on water-saturated soil, since saturated ground, not moving air, is what the organism's infective stage demands.\n\nPattern across the stand is then interrogated, because the two candidate causes are predicted to leave different spatial signatures. Drought-driven crown thinning follows gradients of soil depth, water-holding capacity and topographic position across a stand. By contrast, spatial mapping distinguishes expanding disease centres spreading along drainage lines from decline distributed uniformly across the stand, so that the geometry of the affected area becomes diagnostic in its own right.\n\nThe chronology of the episode is reconstructed from the wood itself. Dendrochronological analysis of increment cores dates the onset of radial growth decline in the affected trees. Tree-ring analysis is unable to identify the calendar year in which a tree's growth decline began, a limitation that cannot both hold and permit the dating of decline onset from cores, so the two accounts of what increment series can deliver are irreconcilable. After the onset year has been fixed in this way, the pathologist turns to the question of introduction, and the conclusion drawn is that at least one of two pathways was operative: infested nursery planting stock brought the pathogen onto the site, or contaminated soil carried on forestry machinery and footwear brought it there, and perhaps both did.",
